  "name": "Question 12",
  "problem": "A particle moves with constant acceleration. When $t=0\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $-8i\\,\\text{km/h},$ and when $t=6\\,\\text{h},$ the velocity of the particle is $(4i-18j)\\,\\text{km/h}.$ Calculate the acceleration of the particle.\n\n$a=($\n{{field-1}}$\\,i$\n$-$\n{{field-2}}$\\,j\\,)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}$",
  "worked_solution": "Recall that when a particle moves with constant acceleration, the velocity vector at time $t$ can be found using\n\n$$\nv=u+at,\n$$\n\nwhere $u$ is the initial velocity, and $a$ is the acceleration of the particle.\n\nSubstituting \n\n$$\nu=-8i,\\,v=4i-18j,\\,t=6,\n$$\n\ninto the equation above, we can solve for $a,$ as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}v & =u+at \\\\ 4i-18j & =-8i+a(6) \\\\ 12i-18j & =6a \\\\ a & =\\frac{1}{6}(12i-18j) \\\\  & =(2\\,i-3\\,j)\\,{\\text{km/h}}^{2}.\\end{aligned}\n$$",
